    "https://ripon.edu/career-discovery-tour/": {
      "title": "Career Discovery Tour",
      "description": "Career Discovery Tour |  Ripon College Skip to Main Content About About Ripon Why Ripon Ripon at a Glance Mission and Vision Strategic Plan Leadership Community Resources Ripon College History Ripon, WI Fact Sheet Support Ripon Support Giving Ways to Give Where to Give Impact of Giving Engage Engage Alumni Events Alumni Weekend R Connections Update Information Parents and Families Volunteer Opportunities Oral Histories Project (PCI) Community Resources Community Resources Willmore Center Ceresco Prairie Conservancy Art Galleries Give to Ripon Show your support for the college and its students with a donation. Give to Ripon Academics Overview Overview Areas of Study Master’s Programs Centers and Resources STEM Faculty Directory Endowed Chairs and Professors Course Catalog Academic Calendar Experiential Learning Experiential Learning SOAR Catalyst Curriculum Career Discovery Tour Center for Politics and the People Ripon College Spain Program in Barcelona MSN Direct Entry Lane Library & Archives Lane Library Library Information Library of Things Collections College Archives Research Guides Student Support Services Faculty Resources Heinz Franzen Learning Center Registrar Registrar Dean’s List Transcripts Four-Year Graduation Guarantee Areas of Study Learn more about our areas of study to decide if Ripon is right for you! Areas of Study Admissions Overview Overview Connect With Us Virtual Map Visit Visit On-Campus Visits Campus Events Fall Preview Day Private Group Visits Apply Apply to Ripon First-Year Applicants Graduate Applicants International Applicants Transfer Applicants Home-schooled Applicants Financial Aid Cost and Financial Aid Affordability The Ripon Commitment Net Price Calculator Apply For Financial Aid Billing Scholarships and Types of Aid Military Benefits Four-Year Graduation Guarantee Your First Year Financial Aid Offer Accepted Students Accepted Students Deposit Now Student Status Register for Orientation Accepted Student Events Welcome Week Student Life Overview Overview Living in Ripon Housing & Dining Living Options Ripon College Dining Clubs & Activities Clubs and Organizations Greek Life ROTC Center for Diversity and Inclusion Esports Campus Services Health and Wellness Parking and Transportation Campus Safety Resources MyRipon Portal Harassment/Title IX Bias Related Incident Protocol Hazing News & Events News News Media Contact Events Events Calendar Plan your event Fine Art Events Arts Events Music Events Theatre Events Ceremonies & Special Events Welcome Week Catalyst Day Commencement Alumni Weekend Family Weekend and Homecoming Publications Publications Athletics Career Discovery Tour Experiential Learning SOAR Catalyst Curriculum Career Discovery Tour Center for Politics and the People Ripon College Spain Program in Barcelona MSN Direct Entry Gaining value from Successful Alumni The Career Discovery Tour is a program committed to further expanding student-alumni career networking opportunities. In 2014, the program was recognized with the “Best Student Alumni Programming” award from the Council for Advancement and Support of Education. Offered annually during spring break, the tour offers 12-16 students the opportunity to travel for a week to vibrant cities across the U.S. to gain first-hand knowledge of a variety of careers directly from successful Ripon alumni and friends. Engage Critically Advancement and Student Life staff accompany students on the tour and help to design meeting schedules and flex time that meets the needs of most students. Previous trips have included Washington, D.C., Minneapolis, Chicago, Denver, Boston, New York, Colorado, and Texas. This critical engagement opportunity is made possible with financial support from the Alumni Association Board and other alumni and friends of Ripon College. Volunteer with Us Spring 2026 Tour Career Discovery Tour Seattle March 13-18, 2026 ’26 Seattle Confirmed Hosts Emily Cliff ’17 Emily Cliff ’17 Ripon Major: Chemistry Ripon Minor: Biology Graduate School: PhD Chemistry, University of Washington Industry: Biotechnology Position/Employer: Scientist I at Allen Institute, Seattle Hub for Synthetic Biology BIO: Emily’s career has been a gradual shift from traditional organic chemistry research (with Ripon’s very own Professor Willoughby!), to chemical biology (PhD in Jesse Zalatan’s Lab at UW-Seattle ), and finally into the world of biotechnology and synthetic biology. Synthetic biology allows her to take nature’s blueprints and reimagine them as powerful new biological tools—whether for environmental applications like bioremediation or cutting-edge therapies such as CAR-T. On a day-to-day basis, she splits time between dreaming up clever new designs and getting hands-on in the lab to test them. The balance between mental creativity and hands-on experimentation is refreshing and grounding. LinkedIn Profile Kathy Graunke ’73 Kathy Graunke ’73 Ripon Majors: Chemistry, German Graduate School: Doctor of Medicine, University of Wisconsin-Madison; Master’s of Public Health, Harvard University Industry: Healthcare Position/Employer: Retired Pediatrician BIO: Retiring in 2016, Dr. Graunke spent more than 25years as a general pediatrician in the greater Seattle area. Her previous 10 years in practice were in Boston, Berkeley, CA, and Connecticut. John Hinds ’96 John Hinds ’96 Ripon Major: Music Graduate School: Master of Arts in Organizational Systems, The Leadership Institute of Seattle; Executive MBA, Quantic School of Business & Technology. Industry: Healthcare Leadership Consulting Position/Employer: Talent, Learning & Capability Consultant at CommonSpirit BIO: John is an internal organizational effectiveness consultant to 14 leadership teams at CommonSpirit, a non-profit healthcare system focused on diverse communities and eliminating barriers to healthcare. LinkedIn Profile Kevin Meserve ’10 Kevin Meserve ’10 Ripon Major: Psychology Ripon Minor: Business Management Graduate School: JD, Willamette University Industry: Family Law Position/Employer: Attorney at Connolly, Tacon, and Meserve BIO: Kevin’s practice focuses primarily on family law, divorce, and child custody. He is experienced in civil protection orders, including anti-harassment, domestic violence, and sexual assault. He volunteers with Thurston County Volunteer Legal Services as a mentor attorney for their family law legal clinic. Chip Moore ’75 Willson “Chip” Moore ‘75 Ripon Majors: Political Science Industry: Financial Services, Institutional Investing Position/Employer: Retired (prior: Manulife John Hancock Investments) BIO: With over 40 years in the financial services industry, Chip’s focus was the relational development and management of trusted consultants and advisors serving large institutional employers as well as their trustees and investment boards. Bill Quistorf ’80 Bill Quistorf ’80 Ripon Major: Art Wayland Baptist University ’86:  Bachelor of Science in Aviation Technology Graduate School: Master’s in Systems Management, University of Southern California Industry: Law Enforcement, Aviation Position/Employer: Chief Pilot, Snohomish County Sheriff’s Department (25 years) BIO: Deputy Quistorf’s work with the Snohomish County Helicopter Search and Rescue team helps to locate and rescue victims trapped in avalanches, stranded hikers, and adventurists. In 2004, he helped establish and now chairs the Northwest Regional Aviation Group. Previously, he served 25 years in U.S. Army Aviation. Jason Reiner P’26 Jason Reiner P’26 Education: BS, Central Washington University Industry: Information Systems, Technology Position/Employer: Partner Group Engineering Manager, Microsoft BIO: Jason has over 22 years of experience leading multi-disciplinary teams that provide critical enterprise IT services and support network engineering, service and security engineering, site reliability engineering, and sourcing engineering. His background is in IT infrastructure operations, IT and business strategy alignment, and network technology. LinkedIn Profile Rich Rorem ’81 Rich Rorem ’81 Ripon Majors: Economics, History Graduate School: MBA, Seattle University Industry: Finance, Executive Coaching Position/Employer: Retired, Deloitte Consulting More to come! Sara Sisco ’11 Sara Sisco ’11 Ripon Major: Politics and Government Ripon Minor: National Security Studies Graduate School: Master’s in Non-Profit Leadership, Seattle University Industry: Non-Profit Management Position/Employer: Senior Manager – Mobility Education and Outreach, Hopelink BIO: Sara is a nonprofit leader guiding mobility outreach programs that support equitable transportation access in King County. With a master’s in Nonprofit Leadership and a background in community engagement, she focuses on empowering staff, strengthening partnerships, and improving how communities navigate transit. LinkedIn Profile Frank Smoll ’63 Frank Smoll ’63 Ripon Majors: Biology, Physical Education Graduate School: PhD Kinesiology, University of Wisconsin – Madison Industry: Sport Psychology Position/Employer: Professor Emeritus, University of Washington BIO: Dr. Smoll’s research focused on coaching behaviors and the psycho-social effects of competition on children and adolescents. He published more than 145 scientific articles and book chapters, and he is author/co-author of 25 books and manuals on youth sports. Professor Smoll made over 150 presentations at scientific/professional conferences and invited colloquia. He conducted more than 575 psychologically-oriented coaching clinics and workshops for parents of young athletes in the United States and Canada. Laura Yuenger White ’04 Laura Yuenger White ’04 Ripon Major: Psychology Graduate School: Master’s in Industrial and Organizational Psychology, University of Tennessee Chattanooga Industry: Commercial Real Estate Position/Employer: Chief of Staff and Director of Business Operations, CBRE BIO: Laura is a Swiss Army knife-type generalist who never committed to one career path, mostly because decision paralysis is her superpower. Instead, she helps people, projects, and teams get unstuck. LinkedIn Profile The Career Discovery Tour is a donor-funded initiative started by former President Zach Messitte and the Ripon College Alumni Board of Directors. The program is designed to help students learn the value of the liberal arts from successful alumni and provide experiences related to living and working in a city. Student participants also gain networking skills. More than 150 students have participated since spring 2013. The most influential knowledge I gained during the trip was that it doesn’t matter what major you graduate with. Rather it is the skills that you hone during your college years that make an impact on your career. That for a member of civil services the best skill she learned was conduct. It really shows how most alumni use the skills they learned rather than the degree they earned. Isaac Tomaschefsky ’24 Participant in 2023 trip to Washington, D.C. This trip provided students with an abundant experience of professional environments while also introducing us to successful Ripon College alumni. In particular, these site visits illustrate the valuable traits and mentalities required to lead a professional career. The visits also demonstrated the power of Ripon College’s liberal arts education in developing critical-thinking skills that are applicable in the workplace and help separate Ripon College graduates from others. Robert Rosner ’19 Participant in the 2019 trip to Texas The Career Discovery Tour let me see a glimpse of what it is like to live in another state. We were able to experience New York traffic, Boston museums and many more unique aspects of life in different cities. We met with numerous Ripon College alumni, and it was insightful to see where they ended up in their careers. Some are in careers which were related to their majors, while some of them had careers which do not correlate specifically to what they studied in college. Hanna Kashchei ’25 Participant in the 2024 trip to the East Coast Previous Next Engage with Ripon College Looking to give back to Ripon? Keep in touch! We can’t wait to hear from you. Get involved Alumni Events Volunteer Opportunities Quick Links MyRipon Portal Red Hawks Athletics Lane Library Make a Gift Admissions Request Information Visit Apply Resources Campus Map Course Catalog Connect With Us Jobs at Ripon Consumer Information Ripon College 300 W. Seward St. Ripon, WI 54971 P: 920-748-8115 E: [email protected] Copyright © 2026. All Rights Reserved. Privacy Policy ."
    },
    "https://ripon.edu/academics/library/library-services/faculty-resources/": {
      "title": "Faculty Resources",
      "description": "Faculty Resources |  Ripon College Skip to Main Content About About Ripon Why Ripon Ripon at a Glance Mission and Vision Strategic Plan Leadership Community Resources Ripon College History Ripon, WI Fact Sheet Support Ripon Support Giving Ways to Give Where to Give Impact of Giving Engage Engage Alumni Events Alumni Weekend R Connections Update Information Parents and Families Volunteer Opportunities Oral Histories Project (PCI) Community Resources Community Resources Willmore Center Ceresco Prairie Conservancy Art Galleries Give to Ripon Show your support for the college and its students with a donation. Give to Ripon Academics Overview Overview Areas of Study Master’s Programs Centers and Resources STEM Faculty Directory Endowed Chairs and Professors Course Catalog Academic Calendar Experiential Learning Experiential Learning SOAR Catalyst Curriculum Career Discovery Tour Center for Politics and the People Ripon College Spain Program in Barcelona MSN Direct Entry Lane Library & Archives Lane Library Library Information Library of Things Collections College Archives Research Guides Student Support Services Faculty Resources Heinz Franzen Learning Center Registrar Registrar Dean’s List Transcripts Four-Year Graduation Guarantee Areas of Study Learn more about our areas of study to decide if Ripon is right for you! Areas of Study Admissions Overview Overview Connect With Us Virtual Map Visit Visit On-Campus Visits Campus Events Fall Preview Day Private Group Visits Apply Apply to Ripon First-Year Applicants Graduate Applicants International Applicants Transfer Applicants Home-schooled Applicants Financial Aid Cost and Financial Aid Affordability The Ripon Commitment Net Price Calculator Apply For Financial Aid Billing Scholarships and Types of Aid Military Benefits Four-Year Graduation Guarantee Your First Year Financial Aid Offer Accepted Students Accepted Students Deposit Now Student Status Register for Orientation Accepted Student Events Welcome Week Student Life Overview Overview Living in Ripon Housing & Dining Living Options Ripon College Dining Clubs & Activities Clubs and Organizations Greek Life ROTC Center for Diversity and Inclusion Esports Campus Services Health and Wellness Parking and Transportation Campus Safety Resources MyRipon Portal Harassment/Title IX Bias Related Incident Protocol Hazing News & Events News News Media Contact Events Events Calendar Plan your event Fine Art Events Arts Events Music Events Theatre Events Ceremonies & Special Events Welcome Week Catalyst Day Commencement Alumni Weekend Family Weekend and Homecoming Publications Publications Athletics Faculty Resources Lane Library Library Information Library of Things Collections College Archives Research Guides Student Support Services Faculty Resources Heinz Franzen Learning Center Looking for Course Reserves ? When Should I Consult a Librarian? Academic resources are constantly changing, and it can be hard to stay on top of the latest information tools. Ripon College librarians offer faculty research consultations to help with professional research needs. We are happy to meet with you either in person or on the phone to explain the newest resources, update your research skills, or help design search strategies should you hit a research wall. Some situations in which a research consultation is available include: When you are unable to track down a specific item When you would like to learn advanced search techniques for specific databases or online research When your search strategy has yielded no useful results When you have exhausted your normal resources and do not know where to look next This service is intended to aid professors who hit a research roadblock; it is not a substitute for individual research or research generally performed by assistants. Library Instruction Overview Each library instruction session is designed to help students: Successfully complete the research for the assignments you have given Improve their information literacy skills to become productive, socially responsible citizens Planning a session: Strategic timing Is the session occurring at the point of need? Will your students have enough time to finish the assignment if they need to use interlibrary loan? Preparation What are your goals for the session? What are the vital research skills/strategies/concepts students should acquire? Which resources, scholarship, and/or methodology should be emphasized? Do the assignment and the library resources complement each other? Scheduling If you’re interested in bringing your class to the library, check our up-to-date calendar below for room availability. After you’ve found a time that works for you, email Ermine Algaier to set up the session. What can we Cover? Will your students need to select and narrow a topic? (10-30 minutes) Brainstorming a topic based on assignment criteria Identifying keywords, synonyms and controlled vocabulary on their topic Browsing relevant resources for background information Articulating a research question or thesis statement Will students be using library catalogs (local and/or beyond) to do searches on their topic? (10-30 minutes) Searching for books/media by words/phrase vs. subject Finding relevant subject links Interpreting results lists, marking and saving relevant items Using advanced search functions to manipulate publication format, date, language, etc. Using call numbers to locate items on library shelves Requesting material from other libraries via ILL Recognizing e-resources (e-books, web resources) within catalogs Will students be required to distinguish between primary vs. secondary materials? (10-30 minutes) Defining primary and secondary sources Developing strategies to locate primary materials Discussing the contributions of primary and secondary literature to disciplinary knowledge building Will students need to identify and search relevant research databases effectively? (15-60 minutes) Determining the nature and scope of literature indexed Searching with natural language keywords and phrases Using the database thesaurus or controlled vocabulary to search Evaluating results lists for relevance Checking for full-text availability and making ILL requests Revising search strategies for related or more focused results Marking, emailing, exporting, saving and printing results Will students need to understand the difference between scholarly and popular publications? (10-20 minutes) Discussing scholarly vs. popular publishing procedures Determining the scope, purpose and target audiences Will students need to identify and evaluate scholarly and/or popular Internet resources? (15-30 minutes) Recognizing and distinguishing between fee-based and free websites Using advanced Google search Analyzing Internet site organization and search capabilities Determining the scope, audience and currency of information Determining the authority, accuracy and objectivity of sources Analyzing the relevance of information for an information need Will students need to learn how to track down scholarship featured in bibliographies and references? (10-20 minutes) Recognizing which elements of a citation to use for tracking purposes Choosing an appropriate venue to locate cited materials (catalogs, databases, Google, etc.) Will students need to learn about documentation and plagiarism? (10-20 minutes) Recognizing elements of a citation Distinguishing book, article and media citations in various styles Understanding why citations differ from style to style Will students be required to use bibliographic manager software to manage information? (15-60 minutes) Becoming familiar with the capabilities of Zotero Learning to import citations from databases, catalogs and websites Creating records for resources not included in licensed databases and library catalogs Editing records and creating annotations Formatting bibliographies Will students need to know the physical layout of Lane Library? (10-30 minutes) Becoming comfortable with finding physical reference materials, periodicals, DVDs, etc. Developing a sense of how materials are shelved Learning about service points (circulation, reserves, research help Instruction Session Options Multiple sessions in the library or classroom Multiple sessions allow students to incrementally build their information literacy skills. One-time instruction in the library A one-time session in the library at the beginning of the research process can give your students a good foundation for completing a well-researched project. Instruction in the classroom These sessions can last from 15 minutes up to the entire class period depending on the topics addressed. One-on-one assistance with specific projects Students can be required to meet with a librarian as part of a research project. Student-initiated meetings with librarian Librarians are available to work with students from 7:30 a.m. to 5 p.m. Monday through Friday. Students may drop by our offices or email to set up an appointment. Feel free to include our contact information in your syllabi. Library Liaisons What can your library liaison do for you? Serve as a primary contact between the library and your department to answer questions, resolve problems, interpret library policy, and provide information about library resources Assist with acquisitions and collection development Provide one-on-one orientation to new and experienced faculty members Gather information about new areas of research, courses and programs in your department Be aware of curriculum changes and their impact on the library’s collections and services Department Liaisons Extension Art History Ermine Algaier x8750 Art/Studio Art Ermine Algaier x8750 Biology Andrew Prellwitz x8752 Business Andrew Prellwitz x8752 Chemistry Andrew Prellwitz x8752 Communication Ermine Algaier x8750 Computer Science Andrew Prellwitz x8752 Economics Andrew Prellwitz x8752 Education Andrew Prellwitz x8752 English Ermine Algaier x8750 Environmental Studies Andrew Prellwitz x8752 Exercise Science Andrew Prellwitz x8752 Global Studies and Languages Ermine Algaier x8750 History Ermine Algaier x8750 Mathematics Andrew Prellwitz x8752 Military Science Andrew Prellwitz x8752 Music Ermine Algaier x8750 Philosophy Ermine Algaier x8750 Physics Andrew Prellwitz x8752 Politics and Government Andrew Prellwitz x8752 Psychology Andrew Prellwitz x8752 Religion Ermine Algaier x8750 Sociology Andrew Prellwitz x8752 Theatre Ermine Algaier x8750 Women's, Gender, and Sexuality Studies Ermine Algaier x8750 Course Reserves Course reserves are located near the circulation desk on a first-come, first-served basis. Each semester, the library creates a course reserve for every class using materials currently in our collection. The library automatically purchases and places required textbooks for all Catalyst and 100-level courses on reserve each semester. For all other courses, if we do not own the required materials, you will need to submit a purchase request well in advance of the date needed for your class. While the standard turnaround for purchasing and processing is one week, please anticipate longer wait times during periods of high volume at the beginning of the semester. If you have personal items that you would like to place on reserve, please bring your materials to the circulation desk and fill out a Course Reserves form. If you would like to submit a purchase request, put a personal item on reserves, or have any questions, please contact Ermine Algaier ( [email protected] ). X/Twitter This field is for validation purposes and should be left unchanged. Instructor's Name (Required) First Last Email Department (Required) Course Number (Required) *Include section, if applicable. Course Title (Required) List the title, author, and call number of all library items to be placed on reserve here: (Required) Checkout Period (Required) 3 hour checkout (no overnight) 1 day checkout 3 day checkout Submit Quick Links MyRipon Portal Red Hawks Athletics Lane Library Make a Gift Admissions Request Information Visit Apply Resources Campus Map Course Catalog Connect With Us Jobs at Ripon Consumer Information Ripon College 300 W. Seward St. Ripon, WI 54971 P: 920-748-8115 E: [email protected] Copyright © 2026. All Rights Reserved. Privacy Policy ."
